Make terminal OSC 52 copy reach the system clipboard

Programs running in the integrated terminal (tmux, vim, Claude Code...) copy by
emitting OSC 52. xterm's clipboard addon is always loaded and hands the payload
to IClipboardService.writeText(text, 'clipboard') (see xtermTerminal.ts), but
BrowserClipboardService files *any* typed write into an in-memory map and never
touches navigator.clipboard, so on web the copy silently does nothing.

'clipboard' is not an alternate clipboard, it is the system one, so let it fall
through. 'selection' (OSC 52 's'/'p', the X11 primary selection) keeps the
in-memory behaviour since browsers have no equivalent.

Only the write path is changed. readText('clipboard') deliberately keeps
returning the in-memory value, so an OSC 52 read query (ESC]52;c;? BEL) cannot
be used by a remote program to exfiltrate the user's clipboard back into the
terminal as input.

This needs a secure context (HTTPS or localhost); without one
navigator.clipboard is undefined and the execCommand fallback cannot run,
because OSC 52 arrives asynchronously with no user gesture attached.

To reproduce: open a terminal and run
  printf '\033]52;c;%s\a' "$(printf hello | base64)"
then paste. Before this patch the clipboard is unchanged.

Fixes the web half of https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/issues/318254 -- drop
this patch once that lands upstream.
